WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.029 --> 00:00:06.438
Guess the first thing I'd like to just ask you is how are you doing?

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:12.889
I'm doing great. So what what's great about what what you're doing? What's so good about your life?

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:20.268
Well right now I'm in the process of putting together my next stand-up comedy special

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:25.789
So I'm at the process now where I've actually put together a full new hour of material

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.510
since my Netflix special which came out in October so

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.899
That's that's great for me. It's that's always a

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
a relaxing moment because it's very difficult to put that hour together.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.650
And so it's and so how do you go about doing that?

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:47.389
A lot of writing a lot of performing a lot of reading a lot of going over notes a lot of

00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:50.629
examining material a lot of reviewing

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:57.409
Sets and trying to find out what I like and what I don't like it's a long and brutal process system

00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:02.898
It's the most fun but also the most difficult part of stand-up is the creation of new material

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:07.339
So how many hours do you think you put in of work to do an hour's worth stand-up?

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:08.800
Any idea?

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.250
That's a really good question

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:17.839
It's usually about I can do I can create a solid ten minutes a month. That's usually what it is

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.990
So it takes me six months to do an hour and in that six months on an average week I'll do

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:28.788
Eight or nine sets so that's eight or nine

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:33.169
either half-hour or hours of material

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.749
Sometimes 15 minutes usually an hour depending upon where I'm working and how many other people are on the show

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.469
And then a lot of time writing

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.189
So you're doing those sets in front of live audiences all the time? Yes.

00:01:46.469 --> 00:01:50.119
Yeah, you have to. Let's, that's the weird thing about stand-up comedy

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:55.729
It seems to be that it's it's not something that you can do in a vacuum

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.300
It has to actually be done. It actually it has to come alive in front of the audience

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.880
Like I can I can write in a vacuum,

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.520
I can write alone, I can, uh

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.700
contemplate, go over my material, review,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.870
edit, I could all sorts of things by myself, but it really doesn't come alive until it's in front of an audience

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.780
Yeah, well, I guess it's not so easy to figure out what's funny

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.080
You kind of hope that people will laugh

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:28.780
yeah, it's that, but it's also there's a state of mind that you only really achieve when you're performing in front of an audience and

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.870
You can try to recreate it, but it'll be fake if you try to do it on your own

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:38.800
I don't write I don't write in joke form, like I don't write the way I say it on stage

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.520
I write in sort of a conceptual form.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:49.980
I write in an essay form and then I sort of extract things that I think are funny out of that, but they really only

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:55.130
find their true the true way I'm going to do them

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:59.870
I only find that in front of an audience because it's like when the when I'm in front of an audience

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:07.009
Then it becomes clear to me how I should and shouldn't say things based in part on how they're reacting

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.200
And based in part on how I feel

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.719
when I'm performing the idea

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:16.820
Like I find where the the fat of the bit is, and that's where you kind of

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.600
appreciate economy of words and you know what to edit out, and

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.660
what to elaborate on, what people aren't totally

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.800
understanding, and what maybe is

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.560
over-explained, and all that stuff kind of comes together in front of an audience

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:38.060
So the essays that you're writing or the writing that you're doing like are they on serious topics? Are they on things you're thinking about

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.920
philosophically or are you trying specifically to be funny or you just trying to get some thoughts down?

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.460
You know about the way you're thinking about the world?

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.340
Both

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.280
you know, it's like the ideas

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.800
if, it's

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.800
See I would say that stand-up comedy, at least the way I do it, it comes in three forms

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:02.330
Like there's three steps. In the beginning, you're really just trying to get laughs, you're fighting for survival out there. You're scared

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:09.530
That's in the early days of your career. Then you start doing what you think is funny, like things that would make you laugh

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.080
But then in stage three

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.220
you start

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.089
trying to make ideas funny and you try to

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:22.160
cleverly introduce ideas into people's heads that maybe they wouldn't

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:30.220
entertain without the humor aspect of it. And so when I write, if I write on a subject, whatever the subject might be,

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.640
I write without thinking oh, I have to make each word funny or I have to make each sentence funny

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.760
I write just what are my thoughts on this subject and then along the way I find

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:45.979
irony and I find

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.100
ridiculous

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.980
perceptions and all the things that lead to stand-up comedy material

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:53.600
Right, and how

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.100
and I extract those

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:59.040
Right, and how much of their like humor and the whip just occurs to you spontaneously on the stage?

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.420
Sometimes a lot. It depends on the subject

00:05:02.729 --> 00:05:07.669
But it's always a possibility, some of the best lines that I've ever come up with

00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:14.100
in my act to come up with, I come up with on the spot while I'm just talking about things

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.109
Right, well that should be when you're like into the subject and things are going well with the audience

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.530
Yeah, yeah, that's basically how it goes

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:22.800
It's a tricky business

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.720
yeah, it sounds like an extremely tricky business and one way the cost of failure is, is

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.240
humiliation and and and, uh

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.219
emotional pain

00:05:32.219 --> 00:05:34.219
Yeah, it's the worst

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:38.539
There's not that many things that are more embarrassing than like trying to be funny

00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:42.739
Especially if you've put say a hundred hours into one hour of preparation

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.699
Which is less than you're doing and then finding out that you're just not that amusing

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.100
That doesn't sound

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:50.600
